Q1. What is unsupervised learning?
Unsupervised learning discovers patterns, structures, or groups in data without predefined target labels. Examples include clustering, dimensionality reduction, and some anomaly-detection methods.
Q2. What is K-Means?
K-Means is a clustering algorithm that divides numerical data into \(K\) clusters by assigning data points to nearby centroids and iteratively updating those centroids to reduce within-cluster squared distances.
Q3. Why do we use feature scaling with K-Means?
K-Means relies on distances between data points. Features with larger numerical ranges can dominate the distance calculation. Scaling helps features contribute more comparably.
Q4. What is a centroid?
A centroid is the mean position of the data points assigned to a cluster. K-Means updates the centroids during training.
Q5. How do you select the value of K?
I can use the Elbow Method to inspect the decrease in inertia as K increases, along with silhouette score, cluster stability, interpretability, and business requirements.
Q6. What is the difference between K-Means and classification?
Classification learns from labeled examples to predict known categories. K-Means groups observations based on similarity without predefined class labels.
Q7. Can K-Means identify fraudulent transactions?
K-Means can help identify unusual groups or observations that may deserve investigation. However, it does not directly prove fraud.
A real fraud-detection system needs validation against known cases, domain rules, and appropriate evaluation.
Q8. Are cluster IDs meaningful?
No. Cluster IDs such as 0, 1, and 2 are arbitrary identifiers. We interpret clusters by analyzing their characteristics.
